\chapter{Atomic poll and heterogeneous readiness}
\label{ch:43}
\index{Atomic poll and heterogeneous readiness}
\index{Channels!poll}

\OnxCodeTerm{poll} presents several eligible channel or captured-stream operations as one choice. A receive arm binds exactly the payload type of its endpoint. EOF has its own outcome. A blocked send must observe backpressure and cannot commit merely because an earlier probe found room. Captured stdout can participate alongside an ordinary channel without converting the ordinary payload to bytes.

\section{Exercise competing arms}
Use one normal typed sender and one captured byte-stream receiver. Make each become ready first in separate tests. Check the bound payload in the selected arm, and exercise a stream that closes before returning data. If a timer is also present, verify the deadline case in a separate run that does not depend on the scheduler's ordering for simultaneous readiness. Runtime registration and withdrawal belong to the atomic wait. A handwritten loop of \OnxCodeTerm{try\_} calls can have different scheduling and selection behavior.

\section{Add the byte and the signed value}
In the mixed-stream example a captured cat process receives one byte with all bits set. The program closes its input and also queues the signed integer minus 42 on a separate channel. Consider the loop's accumulator, initially zero. If the integer receive wins first, the accumulator becomes minus 42. That arm marks the integer as accounted for; it does not close the byte stream. A later receive from stdout binds an unsigned byte, and the explicit conversion supplies 255. The total is now 213. If the byte wins first, the intermediate total is 255 and the final total is still 213. Treating the byte as a signed minus one would produce a different answer.

Why does the byte arm leave its stream marked open after receiving the only byte? At the interface, a byte reports data, not whether there will be a next byte. The example waits for the separate closed outcome, then stops offering that endpoint. Conversely, the ordinary channel is used for exactly one value by the application; it need not wait for a close to know that its part of the sum is complete. The two flags therefore record different facts.

On the successful path stderr closes without data and the program prints the total, then joins the process. The two possible intermediate totals are a useful test of the argument: neither requires that stdout become ready before the integer, nor that both transfers commit in a single poll.

The successful stdin send arm prints its own acknowledgement. This records enqueueing the byte; it is the later stdout receive, not that acknowledgement, that demonstrates the echo.

